    ("gov_m8_32", "Colonial History: 1908 Lagos Water Rate Protest", "2018", "The 1908 mass agitation in Lagos against municipal water rate taxation was spearheaded by nationalist leader:", ["Herbert Macaulay (the Wizard of Kirsten Hall)", "Dr. Nnamdi Azikiwe", "Chief Obafemi Awolowo", "Sir Ahmadu Bello"], 0, "Herbert Macaulay mobilized the market women and traditional rulers of Lagos against colonial municipal water rates, establishing modern mass agitation.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_33", "Constitutional History: Clifford 1922 Elected Seats", "2018", "Under the 1922 Clifford Constitution, how were the 4 elective seats distributed in the Legislative Council?", ["3 seats for Lagos and 1 seat for Calabar", "2 seats for Lagos, 1 for Enugu, 1 for Kaduna", "4 seats for Lagos only", "1 seat each for North, South, East, and West"], 0, "The elective principle applied only to the coastal towns: 3 members represented the Municipality of Lagos and 1 represented Calabar.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_34", "Constitutional History: 1946 Richards Unification", "2018", "For the first time in colonial history, representatives from Northern and Southern Nigeria sat together in the same central legislative council under the:", ["1946 Richards Constitution", "1914 Nigerian Council", "1922 Clifford Constitution", "1954 Lyttelton Constitution"], 0, "The 1946 Richards Constitution integrated Northern Nigerian representatives into the central Legislative Council in Lagos.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_35", "Constitutional History: 1951 Regional Executive Councils", "2018", "Under the 1951 Macpherson Constitution, the regional executive bodies were known as:", ["Regional Executive Councils (chaired by the Lieutenant-Governor)", "Provisional Ruling Councils", "State Executive Councils", "Supreme Military Councils"], 0, "Each region had an Executive Council consisting of official colonial members and Nigerian ministers appointed from the Regional Assembly.", "Medium"),
    ("gov_m8_36", "Constitutional History: 1954 Federal Judiciary", "2018", "The 1954 Lyttelton Constitution established the Federal Supreme Court of Nigeria presided over by the:", ["Federal Chief Justice", "Governor-General", "Lord Chancellor of England", "Premier of Western Nigeria"], 0, "The 1954 constitution created a Federal Supreme Court and regional High Courts, establishing Nigeria's federal judiciary.", "Medium"),
    ("gov_m8_37", "Constitutional History: 1957 Regional Independence", "2018", "When the Western and Eastern Regions achieved self-government in 1957, the regional governors ceased to:", ["Preside over Regional Executive Council meetings, handing chairmanship to the Regional Premiers", "Wear military uniforms", "Receive salaries from London", "Command regional police forces"], 0, "Self-government in 1957 made the Regional Premier the executive chairman of the regional cabinet, reducing the Governor to a constitutional head.", "Medium"),
    ("gov_m8_38", "Constitutional History: Northern Self-Government 1959", "2018", "The Northern Region achieved internal self-government on:", ["March 15, 1959", "October 1, 1960", "October 1, 1954", "May 29, 1999"], 0, "The Northern Region attained internal self-government on March 15, 1959 under the premiership of Sir Ahmadu Bello.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_39", "First Republic: Creation of Midwest Plebiscite", "2018", "The July 13, 1963 Midwest plebiscite resulted in an overwhelming approval rate of over:", ["89% in favor of creating the new Midwest Region", "51%", "60%", "75%"], 0, "Over 89% of registered voters in Benin and Delta provinces voted 'Yes' to carve out the Midwest Region from the Western Region.", "Medium"),
    ("gov_m8_40", "Military Rule: 1970 3Rs Policy", "2018", "At the conclusion of the Nigerian Civil War in January 1970, General Yakubu Gowon declared the policy of:", ["'No Victor, No Vanquished' anchored on Reconciliation, Reconstruction, and Rehabilitation (the 3Rs)", "War Against Indiscipline", "Total Nationalization", "Operation Feed the Nation"], 0, "Gowon's 3Rs program facilitated reintegration and post-war socio-economic reconstruction across the former Eastern Region.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_41", "Military Rule: Operation Feed the Nation", "2018", "The national agricultural campaign launched by the Obasanjo military regime in 1976 to achieve food self-sufficiency was:", ["Operation Feed the Nation (OFN)", "Green Revolution", "Agricultural Development Programme (ADP)", "Directorate of Food, Roads and Rural Infrastructure (DFRRI)"], 0, "OFN encouraged all Nigerians, students, and institutions to cultivate farms and increase local food production.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_42", "Second Republic: Green Revolution", "2018", "The major agricultural program launched by President Shehu Shagari in 1980 to replace OFN was the:", ["Green Revolution Programme", "Operation Feed the Nation", "MAMSER", "National Directorate of Employment"], 0, "The Green Revolution aimed to modernize agriculture and attain food self-sufficiency during the Second Republic.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_43", "Military Rule: DFRRI", "2018", "The Directorate of Food, Roads and Rural Infrastructure (DFRRI) was established in 1986 by the Babangida regime to:", ["Open up rural feeder roads, provide rural water boreholes, and electrify agrarian communities", "Manage federal airports", "Conduct university admissions", "Collect petroleum export revenue"], 0, "DFRRI transformed rural infrastructure across Nigeria through massive feeder road construction, rural water schemes, and electrification.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_44", "Military Rule: MAMSER", "2018", "The Directorate for Social Mobilisation (MAMSER) established in 1987 under Prof. Jerry Gana aimed to:", ["Mobilize citizens for self-reliance, economic recovery, political awareness, and civic duties", "Train military combat pilots", "Construct international expressways", "Administer national external examinations"], 0, "MAMSER conducted massive civic education campaigns to build a politically conscious and productive Nigerian citizenry.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_45", "Public Administration: National Orientation Agency", "2018", "In 1993, MAMSER was merged with other public enlightenment organs to form the permanent agency known as the:", ["National Orientation Agency (NOA)", "Federal Character Commission", "National Youth Service Corps", "Public Complaints Commission"], 0, "The National Orientation Agency (NOA) is Nigeria's premier civic orientation body promoting national unity, core values, and patriotism.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m8_46", "Military Rule: National Electoral Commission 1987", "2018", "The first Chairman of the National Electoral Commission (NEC) appointed by General Babangida in 1987 was:", ["Prof. Eme Awa", "Prof. Humphrey Nwosu", "Prof. Okon Uya", "Chief Michael Ani"], 0, "Prof. Eme Awa chaired NEC from 1987 until 1989 when Prof. Humphrey Nwosu succeeded him.", "Medium"),
    ("gov_m8_47", "Fourth Republic: Anti-Corruption Courts", "2018", "In Nigeria's judicial hierarchy, appeals from the Code of Conduct Tribunal (CCT) lie directly to the:", ["Court of Appeal", "Federal High Court", "State High Court", "Supreme Court of Nigeria directly"], 0, "Section 246(1)(a) of the 1999 Constitution establishes that appeals from the Code of Conduct Tribunal go directly to the Court of Appeal.", "Medium"),
